istan, around the city of Merv.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/margrave-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Margrave of Brandenburg</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Margrave of Brandenburg was the ruler of the March of Brandenburg in north-eastern Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/margrethe-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/margrethe-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Margrethe II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Margrethe II was Queen of Denmark from 1972 until her abdication on 14 January 2024 in favour of her son, Frederik X.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/margus-of-keryneia"><span class="enc-name">Margus of Keryneia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Margus of Keryneia was the first sole general (strategos) of the Achaean League, elected in 255 BCE when the league replaced its two joint generals with one.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mari"><span class="enc-name">Mari</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mari was an ancient city on the middle Euphrates in present-day Syria, a major trading centre in the early second millennium BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maria-beatrice-d-este-duchess-of-massa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/maria-beatrice-d-este-duchess-of-massa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Maria Beatrice d'Este, Duchess of Massa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maria Beatrice d'Este (1750–1829) was heiress of the Duchy of Massa and Carrara through her mother, Maria Teresa Cybo-Malaspina, and of the Este dukes of Modena through…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maria-carolina-of-austria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/maria-carolina-of-austria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Maria Carolina of Austria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maria Carolina of Austria (1752–1814) was queen of Naples and Sicily as the wife of Ferdinand IV, whom she married in 1768.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maria-de-molina"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/maria-de-molina.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">María de Molina</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">María de Molina was Queen of Castile as the wife of Sancho IV and twice served as regent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maria-ii-of-portugal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/maria-ii-of-portugal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Maria II of Portugal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maria II was Queen of Portugal from 1826 to 1828 and from 1834 to 1853.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maria-ii-zaccaria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/maria-ii-zaccaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Maria II Zaccaria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maria II Zaccaria was a Genoese noblewoman, widow of Pedro de San Superano, who ruled the Principality of Achaea from 1402 to 1404.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/maria-komnene-queen-of-jerusalem"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/maria-komnene-queen-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Maria Komnene, Queen of Jerusalem</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Maria Komnene was a Byzantine princess, great-niece of Emperor Manuel I, who married King Amalric I of Jerusalem in 1167.</span></a></li></ul>
